CHAPTER TWO: LITERATURE REVIEW2.1 IntroductionThe literature review provides a comprehensive analysis of the computational and pedagogical frameworks used to track, evaluate, and optimize student performance within technical higher education courses. This chapter establishes the structural foundations of Educational Data Mining (EDM), examines the critical challenge of algorithmic opacity, explores tree-based ensemble methods, and evaluates Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) frameworks tailored for diagnostic educational reporting.  2.2 Conceptual Review2.2.1 Educational Data Mining and Learning AnalyticsEducational Data Mining (EDM) and Learning Analytics (LA) represent overlapping but distinct disciplines focused on extracting actionable insights from academic data. EDM focuses on the development and application of machine learning, data mining, and statistical patterns to process large-scale, multi-feature student interaction logs. Conversely, Learning Analytics focuses on the operational application of these models, designing human-centric dashboards to help instructors optimize instructional environments. In an intelligent tutoring architecture, EDM supplies the predictive models, while LA translates those predictions into visible student insights.  2.2.2 Data Analytics Tasks and Techniques in Intelligent Tutoring SystemsIntelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) leverage specific data analytics techniques to simulate personalized human guidance at scale:  Predictive Modeling (Classification and Regression): Serves as the baseline engine for state tracking, mapping a student's interaction history into categorical classes (e.g., pass/fail classification) or continuous competence variables.  Clustering: Groups diverse student populations into distinct behavioral cohorts based on learning velocity, cognitive efficiency, and interaction frequency.  Association Rule Mining: uncovers hidden relationships between distinct curriculum units, identifying hidden prerequisite dependencies and paths toward multi-concept mastery.  Outlier and Anomaly Detection: Flags abnormal student behaviors, helping to separate genuine cognitive breakthroughs from lucky guessing or sudden slips due to fatigue.  2.2.3 The Problem of Model Opacity (“Black Box” Algorithms)Modern student modeling frequently encounters the problem of model opacity. While deep learning models achieve high accuracy in predicting student failure, they function as "black boxes" whose inner parameters are masked within high-dimensional spaces. This structural opacity prevents instructors from seeing why a student is flagged as "at-risk". Without clear indications of specific concept vulnerabilities or prerequisite gaps, these numbers cannot provide actionable remediation pathways.  2.2.4 Overview of Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost Algorithm)Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) is a highly scalable, optimized tree-boosting machine learning library. It builds an ensemble of weak decision trees sequentially, where each new tree model minimizes a regularized objective function containing a specialized training loss parameter and an explicit model complexity penalty. XGBoost handles data sparsity effectively through built-in sparsity-aware split finding algorithms, making it well-suited for processing real-time student multiple-choice log matrices.  2.2.5 Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) TechniquesExplainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) comprises methodologies designed to open up complex machine learning models, translating hidden algorithmic weights into human-interpretable diagnostics. In educational platforms, XAI bridges the gap between predictive power and instructional transparency. It ensures that automated risk assessments can be understood and verified by educators and students alike.  2.2.6 SHAP (Shapley Additive exPlanations)SHAP (Shapley Additive exPlanations) is a game-theoretic framework that explains individual machine learning predictions by calculating the additive contribution of each input feature to a given model outcome. Rooted in cooperative game theory, SHAP computes the exact marginal contribution of each operational feature across all possible feature subset combinations. This delivers local accuracy and consistency guarantees, ensuring that specific student behaviors (such as response duration) are credited correctly without the statistical bias common in legacy global permutation approaches.  2.3 Empirical Review2.3.1 Review of Knowledge Tracing and Student Modeling TechniquesStudent capability modeling began with psychometric frameworks like Bayesian Knowledge Tracing (BKT) and Item Response Theory (IRT). BKT treats mastery as a binary latent state updated via a Hidden Markov Model, but it assumes different skills are completely independent. IRT measures capability as a continuous parameter ($\theta$), but its standard formulations operate under a static ability assumption, which fails to track real-time learning updates or cognitive changes during active testing sessions.  2.3.2 Tree-Based Methods for Student Performance PredictionEmpirical studies show that tree-based ensemble methods match or exceed deep sequential networks when processing structured behavioral data, such as question logs and response timestamps. Researchers have applied random forests and gradient boosted trees to raw log files, demonstrating that tree-based ensembles handle mixed-variable distributions, missing interaction arrays, and explicit feature thresholds efficiently without suffering from the exploding gradient limitations of legacy architectures.  2.3.3 Deep Learning Approaches in Educational Data MiningDeep learning in student tracking expanded with Deep Knowledge Tracing (DKT), which uses Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) blocks to process interaction histories. While these architectures successfully capture non-linear sequence dependencies, they face performance bottlenecks over long interactive trails. This led to self-attentive Transformer models like SAINT and SAKT, which use parallelized multi-head self-attention mechanisms to map dependencies across extended student practice logs.  2.3.4 Review of Ensemble Learning Techniques for Student Performance PredictionEnsemble methodologies optimize predictive stability by combining multiple base models. In educational diagnostics, hybrid ensemble frameworks use boosting and bagging methods to pool predictions from varied base learners. This reduces model variance and helps safeguard institutional prediction frameworks from overfitting on small, specialized classroom dataset cohorts.  2.3.5 Comparative Analysis of Traditional vs Modern Predictive ModelsEvaluation AxisTraditional Models (BKT/IRT)Deep Sequential Models (DKT/Transformers)Tree Ensemble Models (XGBoost)State DynamicsStatic or simple binary transition steps.  Continuous, complex high-dimensional mappings.  Multi-threshold tabular logic trees.  Computational OverheadLow matrix footprint.  High GPU resource requirements.  Balanced, optimized concurrency runtime.  InterpretabilityHigh parameter transparency.  Low "black box" parameter masking.  Configurable via additive post-hoc XAI engines.  2.3.6 Existing Works in Interpretability in Educational SystemsEarly Approaches (Global Feature Importance): Early research relied on global variable importance rankings. While these methods showed overall dataset trends, they could not explain specific individual predictions or target customized diagnostic needs.  The Shift to Local Explanations (LIME): Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) introduced localized perturbation testing. However, its sampling methods can yield inconsistent results across highly identical interaction entries.  State-of-the-Art (SHAP for Diagnostic Reporting): Modern frameworks use SHAP to build dependable diagnostic dashboards. By combining global dataset tracking with precise local explanations, SHAP can show a student exactly how their recent performance history and response times directly influenced their current mastery score.  2.4 Summary of Literature and Gap AnalysisThe literature documents a clear shift from simple state-based psychometric loops to complex, multi-layered deep sequence tracking models. However, a significant gap remains regarding system deployment context and local implementation. Most advanced model variants are tested on massive, open benchmark datasets (like ASSISTments or EdNet) within high-resource computing setups. There is little empirical documentation on how to adapt these predictive architectures to local university syllabi, such as the NUC MAT101 curriculum in Nigeria, or how to run them on lightweight, local backend engines designed to handle high concurrency spikes under localized server constraints.  2.5 ReferencesChoi, Y., Lee, Y., Cho, J., Baek, J., Kim, B., Cha, Y., Shin, D., Bae, C., & Heo, J. (2020). EdNet: A large-scale hierarchical dataset for educational data mining. Proceedings of the International Conference on Artificial Intelligence in Education, 69–73.  Corbett, A. T., & Anderson, J. R. (1994). Knowledge tracing: Modeling the acquisition of procedural knowledge. User Modeling and User-Adapted Interaction, 4(4), 253–278.  Ghosh, A., Heffernan, N., & Lan, A. S. (2020). Context-aware attentive knowledge tracing. Proceedings of the ACM International Conference on Web Search and Data Mining, 223–231.  Piech, C., Bassen, J., Huang, J., Ganguli, S., Sahami, M., Guibas, L. J., & Sohl-Dickstein, J. (2015). Deep knowledge tracing. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 28, 505–513.  Vaswani, A., Shazeer, N., Parmar, N., Uszkoreit, J., Jones, L., Gomez, A. N., Kaiser, Ł., & Polosukhin, I. (2017). Attention is all you need. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 30, 5998–6008.  CHAPTER THREE: SYSTEM ANALYSIS AND DESIGN3.1 IntroductionThis chapter explains the system analysis, core operational methodologies, and software design engineering used to deploy the Explainable Adaptive Learning platform for MAT101. It details the requirements, dataset attributes, feature extraction logic, model training loops, and post-hoc XAI recommendation frameworks.  3.2 Study Area and Project ContextThe primary study context focuses on mass-enrollment foundational mathematics within Nigerian higher education, specifically targeting the MAT101 (General Mathematics I) freshman curriculum. Given the large student volumes and wide variations in secondary school mathematics preparation, this framework provides a localized, cloud-ready digital assessment setup designed to reduce student failure rates through early automated intervention.  3.3 System Analysis3.3.1 Analysis of Existing SystemConventional institutional testing infrastructure relies on static Computer-Based Testing (CBT) software layouts. These systems select questions linearly using simple randomized pool shuffling. They compress a student's performance record into a single, uninterpretable aggregate score line (e.g., 45% fail) without mapping specific conceptual vulnerabilities, leaving students without actionable pathways for academic remediation.  3.3.2 Analysis of Proposed SystemThe proposed system uses an adaptive testing architecture powered by tree-boosted machine learning and game-theoretic explainability layers. It intercepts student submission entries in real time, computes granular capability profiles across multiple syllabus skill fields, explains predictive paths through SHAP additive metrics, and dynamically shifts question selection to match individual performance levels.  3.4 System Requirements3.4.1 Functional RequirementsAuthentication Module: Manages secure matriculation-number validation and session state configurations.  Dynamic Assessment Interface: Renders question stems, registers multi-choice options, and logs precise interaction timestamps.  XAI Diagnostic Dashboard: Renders interactive SHAP attribution heatmaps detailing topic mastery strengths and prerequisite weaknesses.  Adaptive Recommendation Engine: Intercepts low-competence predictions and adjusts question deployment paths to address underlying skill gaps.  3.4.2 Non-Functional RequirementsLatency: Total backend API response times must remain below 200 milliseconds to prevent interface delay during testing sessions.  Concurrency: The system must handle parallel interaction pipelines from multiple active clients without database deadlocks.  Security: Use parameterized query layers to block SQL injection and protect student performance logs.  3.4.3 Software RequirementsOperating System: Ubuntu Server 22.04 LTS / Windows 11 Enterprise.Backend Pipeline: Python 3.10+, FastAPI framework, XGBoost library, SHAP toolkit.  Frontend Tier: Next.js 14+ framework, TypeScript, TailwindCSS styling.  Database Engine: PostgreSQL relational database.  3.4.4 Hardware RequirementsDevelopment Machine: Intel Core i7 processor (8 cores minimum), 16 GB DDR4 RAM, NVIDIA RTX 3060 GPU with CUDA enabled.  Target Deployment Node: Minimum 2vCPU cloud server instance with 4 GB baseline RAM.3.5 Conceptual FrameworkThe application follows a modular data pipeline split into three core phases:[Student Interaction Log] ──> [Data Preprocessing & Feature Engineering]
