3.6 Dataset Description3.6.1 Source of DatasetThe interaction repository uses open knowledge tracing benchmarks (such as ASSISTments data) re-mapped and calibrated to match the topic layout of the NUC MAT101 curriculum.  3.6.2 Dataset CharacteristicsThe preprocessed tracking dataset contains student execution sequences covering distinct key parameters: student_id (anonymous string tracking), skill_id (syllabus module code mapping), correctness (binary indicator), and response_time (numerical tracking in seconds).  3.7 Data Preprocessing3.7.1 Data CleaningMissing records, incomplete logs, and duplicate session rows are systematically cleared. Response entries with non-logical intervals (such as attempts logged under 1 second or exceeding 10 minutes) are trimmed to eliminate measurement noise.3.7.2 Data TransformationCategorical attributes, such as syllabus skill headers and problem tags, are transformed using standardized label encoding. Correctness parameters are cast to single binary integers, and response durations are scaled to maintain numerical stability during training.  3.7.3 Data AggregationThe transactional interaction data rows are grouped by student ID and sorted chronologically. This converts flat log entries into historical sequence tracking vectors, which capture changing competence patterns across the testing timeframe.  3.8 Feature Engineering3.8.1 Behavioral Feature ExtractionTo capture individual student engagement dynamics, the system generates targeted behavioral features:Cumulative correctness rates per skill block.Rolling accuracy trends across the last five question attempts.Median response time variations compared to global baseline averages.Total historical attempts logged across specific prerequisite topics.  3.8.2 Feature SelectionWe filter out highly collinear structural variables using a variance inflation factor (VIF) test. This keeps only highly predictive, independent behavioral variables within the final training arrays, reducing computational overhead during inference.3.9 Development of the Prediction Model3.9.1 XGBoost AlgorithmThe system uses the extreme gradient boosting algorithm to optimize performance prediction. The structural loss objective function minimizes:  $$\mathcal{L}(\phi) = \sum_{i} l(\hat{y}_i, y_i) + \sum_{k} \Omega(f_k)$$Where $l$ measures the log-loss prediction difference, and $\Omega$ penalizes model complexity to limit overfitting.  3.9.2 Training and Testing SplitThe sequence data matrix is partitioned using an 80/20 train-test split. This separation is stratified by student identity rows to ensure the model evaluates performance predictions on entirely unseen student cohorts.  3.9.3 Model Training ProcedureTraining runs sequentially, where each new tree layer corrects the residual errors left by preceding leaf configurations. Hyperparameter optimization uses grid search strategies, configuring tree depths, learning rates, and child weight thresholds to maximize convergence.  3.9.4 Prediction ProcessDuring live assessment sessions, the FastAPI backend feeds the student's real-time behavioral vectors into the trained model, which outputs a continuous probability score indicating the student's likelihood of correctly answering the next question item.  3.10 Model Evaluation3.10.1 Confusion MatrixThe system establishes a classification confusion matrix to track true positives, false positives, true negatives, and false negatives across all model predictions.3.10.2 Precision, Recall, and F1-ScorePrecision: Measures model accuracy when predicting student success.Recall: Evaluates the system's ability to correctly identify at-risk students who need immediate intervention.F1-Score: Computes the harmonic mean of precision and recall to provide a balanced measure of overall classification performance.3.10.3 ROC Curve and AUC-ROCThe Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic (AUC-ROC) curve serves as our main evaluation metric. The system targets an AUC-ROC threshold above 0.82 to ensure reliable classification performance before live application deployment.  3.11 Explainability and Recommendation Framework3.11.1 SHAP Explainability FrameworkTo fix the "black box" limitation, the framework integrates a TreeSHAP attribution layer. This decomposes individual student performance predictions into clear, additive feature contributions.  3.11.2 Global ExplanationsGlobal SHAP summaries identify overall system trends across the entire student cohort. This highlights which behavioral factors—such as extended response times or gaps in prerequisite topics like Surds—most heavily influence overall student success.3.11.3 Local ExplanationsLocal explanations generate personalized attributions for individual users. If the model predicts a high risk of failure on a topic like Complex Numbers, the local SHAP engine shows the student exactly how much this prediction was driven by their historical performance gaps in Trigonometry.3.11.4 Recommendation Generation ModuleThe recommendation module turns local SHAP insights into automated instructional changes. If a student's mastery score on a target topic drops below 50%, the system halts the current testing path and serves prerequisite questions to help rebuild foundational skills.3.12 System Design3.12.1 System Architecture┌────────────────────────┐       ┌────────────────────────┐
